@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+{"id":"i-have-a-dream","title":"I Have a Dream","author":"Rashmi Bansal","category":"Business & Startups","year":2011,"readTime":"8 min","bigIdea":"Rashmi Bansal's third book profiles social entrepreneurs — Indians who built businesses with a mission beyond profit: education for slum kids, solar power for villages, healthcare for the poor. Her message: you don't have to choose between making money and changing the world — the best social enterprises do both, because a sustainable mission needs a sustainable business. The dreamers in this book prove that India's biggest problems are its biggest business opportunities, and that the entrepreneur's heart matters as much as the entrepreneur's spreadsheet.","lessons":[{"title":"The Dream That Pays: Mission and Margin Together","chapter":"Part 1: The Dreamers","summary":"Bansal's founders refuse the false choice between profit and purpose. The school that charges slum parents a little but delivers real learning, the solar company that sells to villages at a margin — each proves that a mission without a business model dies, and a business without a mission hollows out. The lesson: don't choose between doing good and doing well; design the model where the good IS the business. The most durable social change is the kind that pays for itself, because it can scale without depending on donations forever.","example":"Bansal profiles an education venture that charges the poor a small fee and delivers quality — profitable enough to grow, mission-driven enough to matter. The fee was the discipline; the mission was the fuel.","action":"Redesign one idea as 'mission + margin': who pays, what changes, and how the two reinforce each other? Write the one-line model."},{"title":"The Slow Road: Social Change Doesn't Move at Startup Speed","chapter":"Part 2: The Pace","summary":"These founders measure progress in years, not quarters — winning trust in a village, changing a habit, building a school that survives its founder. Bansal's lesson: the most important problems resist the 'move fast and break things' playbook; they reward patience and trust. The lesson isn't 'go slow' but 'match your pace to the problem': quick wins for product problems, patient compounding for people problems. The founder who treats a community like a market to be hacked will be rejected; the one who treats it like a family to be served earns loyalty no competitor can buy.","example":"A founder Bansal profiles spent years earning a village's trust before her health program took root — the slow phase looked like failure and was actually the entire strategy.","action":"Identify one 'people problem' in your work that needs patience, not speed. Redefine success for it as trust built, not tasks done."},{"title":"The Founder's Fire: Mission Sustains What Money Can't","chapter":"Part 3: The Fuel","summary":"When these ventures faced crises — funding gaps, burnout, betrayal — what kept them going was not the business plan but the fire: a personal connection to the problem. Bansal shows that mission-driven founders persist through failures that purely commercial founders abandon, because the failure isn't just theirs — it's the community's too. The lesson: choose problems you personally care about, because the care is the capital that survives the dark years. The founder whose motivation is deep survives the shallow times; the one whose motivation is shallow quits at the first valley.","example":"One founder, facing collapse, sold his own house to keep the school running — an act no investor spreadsheet explains, only the fire does. The mission was the balance sheet that mattered.","action":"Write the problem you care about most and why it's personal. Re-read it the next time motivation dips — the fire is the fuel."},{"title":"Funding Is a Means: Don't Let the Money Own the Mission","chapter":"Part 4: The Money","summary":"Bansal's founders navigate the grant-versus-business tension: grants can distort (you serve the donor's metrics) and investors can distort (you chase the exit). Her lesson: funding is fuel, not direction — the founder who lets the money set the mission ends up with someone else's dream. The discipline: know your non-negotiables before you raise money, and choose funders who buy your vision rather than renting it. The same applies to any venture: the capital that comes with strings pulls the steering wheel; keep your hand on it.","example":"A founder refused a large grant that required abandoning her core program — the grant would have doubled her budget and halved her mission. The refusal looked foolish; the mission survived.","action":"List your non-negotiables before any funding or big partnership. Refuse any money that would break them — write the refusal criteria now."},{"title":"The Ripple: One Person's Dream Becomes Many Lives","chapter":"Part 5: The Impact","summary":"Bansal's closing portraits show impact as a ripple: the student who becomes a teacher, the villager who becomes a trainer, the community that stops needing the NGO. The lesson: the deepest success metric is not output but independence — have you made yourself unnecessary? The leader, teacher or founder whose work empowers others to stand alone has created the only growth that lasts. The ripple is the proof: when the dream becomes many people's, it was never just yours.","example":"The school that trained its own teachers, the solar company whose technicians were villagers — each built the exit of its own dependency. The founder's greatest achievement was becoming optional.","action":"Define success as independence: what would it look like if your team, students or community stopped needing you? Build one 'exit of dependency' this quarter."},{"title":"The Dream Is the Business Plan: Start With Why You Care","chapter":"Part 6: The Beginning","summary":"Every founder in the book started the same way: with a problem they couldn't ignore, not a market they researched. Bansal's final lesson: the best business plans begin as personal outrage or love, and the plan comes after. The lesson: stop waiting for the perfect pitch; start with the thing you can't stop thinking about. The market research matters later; the care matters first. The dream is not the enemy of the business plan — it is the seed the plan grows from. If you don't dream it first, you'll never plan it properly.","example":"Bansal's founders routinely began with one angry question — 'why do these kids not have schools?' — and the organizations grew from the question, not from a consulting report.","action":"Write the one problem that keeps you awake. That's your seed — sketch the smallest possible version of the answer this week."}],"quotes":["The most durable social change is the kind that pays for itself.","The care is the capital that survives the dark years.","Your greatest achievement is making yourself unnecessary."],"actionPlan":["Write your 'mission + margin' model in one line.","Match your pace to the problem — patience for people problems.","Identify your personal fire and keep it visible.","List your non-negotiables before taking any money.","Start with the problem you can't ignore — sketch the smallest answer."],"tagline":"Social entrepreneurs who made mission pay.","oneLiner":"Rashmi Bansal profiles Indians who built businesses that change lives — proving mission and margin can coexist when the good IS the business.","cover":"assets/covers/i-have-a-dream.jpg","caveat":"The dream-building genre — vision, purpose, scale — has a shadow the books never mention: WeWork India's parent rode the dream of 'elevating the world's consciousness' to a $47 billion valuation and then the dream met accounting, and the valuation became a lesson in what dreams without economics are worth. The caveat for every dreamer: Martin Luther King's dream worked because it was attached to a real movement with real costs and real people. The startup dream works the same way — the vision is fuel, but the business must still be true. Have a dream; also have a P&L. The world pays for outcomes, not for consciousness-raising.","graveLink":"wework-india"}
