+{"id":"make-time","title":"Make Time","author":"Jake Knapp & John Zeratsky","year":2018,"category":"Productivity","cover":"assets/covers/make-time.jpg","readTime":"8 min","tagline":"How to focus on what matters every day — from the designers who built Gmail's inbox and YouTube's binge engine.","oneLiner":"You can't add hours, but you can design them: one Highlight a day, and a phone that works for you, not on you.","bigIdea":"Jake Knapp and John Zeratsky — the Google designers behind time-management systems used by millions — wrote Make Time after realizing their own 'productivity' tools made them busier, not happier. Their system is radically simple: every day, pick ONE Highlight (the thing that matters most), make time for it with a laser (remove distractions), and energize yourself with real breaks (move, eat, sleep, unplug). The infinity pool — the endless feed of distractions — is the enemy; you can't quit it, but you can design around it.","quotes":["Busy-ness is not a virtue; it's a trap.","The Highlight is the one thing you want to make sure happens today.","The infinity pool is the default state of modern life — the battle is to swim against it."],"lessons":[{"title":"The Highlight: One Intention Per Day","chapter":"Highlight","summary":"Every day, choose your Highlight: the one thing that, if it happens, makes the day a success. It can be urgent (a deadline), satisfying (a passion), or joyful (a person) — but it must be ONE, chosen deliberately in the morning, not discovered at midnight. The Highlight turns a reactive day into a designed day.","example":"Knapp and Zeratsky's rule from their own lives: writing days began by picking the single chapter section that mattered most — everything else (email, meetings) was scheduled AROUND it, not before it. Days without a Highlight drifted into the infinity pool.","action":"Tomorrow morning, write your Highlight in one sentence. Ask: 'Is this urgent, satisfying, or joyful?' Put it in your calendar for a fixed time."},{"title":"The Laser: Design Out Distraction","chapter":"Laser","summary":"Once you have a Highlight, you need a laser: specific tactics to keep your attention on it. The authors' arsenal: phone in another room, browser with one tab, the 'busy' calendar block, headphones as a signal, and 'the phone stack' for meetings. The laser isn't willpower — it's design: you arrange the environment so focus is the default.","example":"Their favorite: make your phone boring — delete social apps (or move them off the home screen), turn off every notification except humans. Knapp describes his phone as 'a brick that makes calls,' and his focus doubled.","action":"Pick one laser tactic today: phone in another room during your Highlight, or notifications off for 2 hours. Do it for 5 days straight."},{"title":"Energize: The Body Runs the Mind","chapter":"Energize","summary":"Focus is physical: a tired, hungry, motionless body can't sustain attention. The authors' energy rules: move every 90 minutes (exercise is the 'highlight multiplier'), eat real food (not sugar spikes), sleep 8 hours (the ultimate performance tool), and take genuine breaks — walking, not scrolling, because screens keep your attention working.","example":"Knapp and Zeratsky both noticed their best ideas and focus came after exercise — so they scheduled workouts before hard work, not after. The authors also treat sleep like a competitive advantage, citing their own and others' data that tired days are lost days.","action":"Add one energizer this week: a 10-minute walk before your Highlight, or a fixed bedtime 30 minutes earlier. Track your focus difference."},{"title":"The Infinity Pool: Know Your Enemy","chapter":"The Infinity Pool","summary":"The infinity pool is the endless feed — social media, news, video, the bottomless scroll — engineered by the smartest minds in the world to keep you swimming. The authors' warning: you can't win by willpower alone against products built by thousands of engineers. You win by design: making the pool hard to enter (log out, delete, move) and boring once you're in.","example":"Both authors worked at Google on products that grew the pool (Gmail, YouTube) — which is exactly why they built Make Time: they knew the machinery from the inside, and knew the only defense is environmental.","action":"Make one pool entry harder today: log out, delete the app, or put the phone across the room at night. Make one pool session shorter: set a 10-minute timer."},{"title":"Design Days, Not Decades","chapter":"Daily Systems","summary":"The book's final philosophy: don't chase a perfect system — run daily experiments. Each day, pick a Highlight, a laser tactic, and an energizer; at night, reflect on what worked ('highlight: yes, laser: failed, tomorrow: try the other room'). Small daily design beats grand yearly resolutions every time.","example":"The authors literally rate their days and adjust one variable per day — a personal A/B testing loop. Their finding: people don't fail from lack of ambition; they fail from lack of daily design.","action":"Tonight, write tomorrow's Highlight + one laser tactic + one energizer. Tomorrow night, rate each (1–5) and change exactly ONE thing."},{"title":"Choose One Highlight Each Day and Protect It","chapter":"The Highlight","summary":"Knapp and Zeratsky's simple engine: each morning pick one 'highlight' — the single thing that will make the day count — then build the day around it, starting with it when possible. The highlight converts an infinite to-do list into a finite, winnable game. Busy is not the same as meaningful; the highlight is the meaning.","example":"A manager who starts her day with one deep-work highlight — the report or decision that matters — protects it from the inbox and finishes it; days without a highlight vanish into meetings. The choice of one focus each morning is the whole method.","action":"Every morning for a week, write one highlight sentence: 'Today will be a win if I...' — and do that before checking messages."}],"actionPlan":["Pick a daily Highlight every morning — one sentence, scheduled.","Deploy one laser tactic daily for 5 days.","Add one energizer (walk, food, sleep) before your Highlight.","Make one infinity-pool entry harder today.","Run the daily A/B loop: rate Highlight, laser, energizer — change one thing."],"caveat":"Knapp & Zeratsky's 'make time for what matters' is the sanest focus system of the decade — and Argo AI is the corporate warning: a company that made time brilliantly — a decade of focus on one highlight, self-driving cars, backed by Ford and VW — and the highlight itself turned out to be a $3.6 billion dollar graveyard, because making time for the wrong highlight is still wasting time. The system's 'choose your highlight' step assumes you can pick correctly. Argo picked with total commitment; the market picked differently. Highlight wisely, and re-check the highlight against reality annually.","graveLink":"argo-ai"}
